    35	The maintenance of build systems imposes a considerable overhead
    36	on software development. Since automated quality assurance methods are rarely applied to build specifications, the importance of the
    37	role peer code review plays in the maintenance of build systems is
    38	amplified. Yet prior work shows that the review process for build
    39	systems suffers from a lack of build experts and effective tooling.
    40	To support the understanding of changes to build specifications
    41	(a key stage in the review process), we propose BCIA—an approach
    42	to summarize the impact of changes to build specifications across
    43	the build configuration space. BCIA traverses the paths through
    44	which data and control flow in the prior and updated versions of the
    45	build system to generate an Impact Knowledge Graph (IKG), which
    46	describes the impact of the change across the build configuration
    47	space. We develop BuiScout—a prototype implementation of BCIA
    48	for CMake-based build systems. We use BuiScout to evaluate our
    49	approach through an empirical study of 10,000 change sets that
    50	we mine from ten large open-source projects that span a total of
    51	28 development years. Our findings indicate that BuiScout can
    52	detect an impact that propagates to unmodified parts of the build
    53	system in 77.37% of the studied change sets. These changes impact
    54	a median of 14 unmodified commands, with a median of 95.55% of
    55	the impacted commands per change set appearing in unmodified
    56	files. Our study suggests that dedicated approaches, such as BCIA,
    57	have the potential to alleviate the challenges developers face when
    58	assessing the impact of changes to build systems.
