    98	Prior work [ 4 ] shows that up to 27% of source code changes and 44%
    99	of test code changes also update the build system. This overhead can
   100	impact a large proportion of team members, with up to 79%–89% of
   101	developers being affected [ 4 ]. Moreover, maintaining build systems
   102	for software with a high degree of compile-time variability requires
   103	a concerted effort [5].
   104	As build systems continue to evolve, they become prone to quality decay [ 6 ], which can slow down builds [ 7, 8 ], cause build failures [ 7, 9, 10 ], or even lead to erroneous software behavior [ 11 ].
   105	Despite these risks, systematic and automated quality assurance
   106	practices, such as automated testing, are rarely applied to build
   107	specifications, leaving peer code review as the primary method for
   108	sustaining the quality of build systems. Unfortunately, the changes
   109	to build systems are often not rigorously reviewed due to social
   110	and technical reasons [ 12 ], such as a pervasive lack of expertise in
   111	build systems and interest in their maintenance [12, 13].
   112	In our prior work [ 12 ], practitioners lamented a perceived lack
   113	of tools to support the maintenance of build systems and the review
   114	of changes to build specifications. Understanding the implications
   115	of changes within the complex configuration space of build systems
   116	remains a challenging task [ 13 ]. Change Impact Analysis (CIA) has
   117	shown potential in improving the effectiveness and efficiency of
   118	code reviews in the context of production code by exposing the
   119	impact of changes across the system [ 14 – 17 ]. Yet, automated tools
   120	to facilitate CIA for build systems are scarce.
   121	Inspired by the growing evidence of the benefits of adopting
   122	CIA in the code review cycle, we conjecture that an approach to
   123	conducting CIA on changes to build systems would help when creating and reviewing changes in build systems. Prior work [ 18, 19 ]
   124	proposes tools to illustrate the impact of changes to source code
   125	by tracing their propagation through the build system; however, to
   126	the best of our knowledge, a CIA approach does not yet exist for
   127	changes to build system itself. Such a technique would allow stakeholders to navigate the impact of the changes to the build system
   128	and potentially expose a missing or unintended impact of a change .
   129	Therefore, we propose _BCIA_ —an approach that uses data and
   130	control flow analysis to assess the impact of changes across the
   131	build configuration space. BCIA uses Conditional Definition-Use
   132	(CDU) chains [ 20 ]—an augmented _Definition-Use (DU) chain_ that,
   133	along with the data flow information, implicitly captures control
   134	flow by storing the reachability condition of the definition and use
   135	points. BCIA then infers how change impact propagates within
   136	and across CDU chains based on value- and reachability conditioncontamination patterns, storing this information in an Impact Knowledge Graph (IKG).
   137	To evaluate the applicability of BCIA, we implement _BuiScout_, a
   138	prototype tool for BCIA that analyzes _CMake_ build systems, which
   139	are renowned for the complexity of their maintenance [ 21 ]. We
   140	then use BuiScout to conduct an empirical study of 10 _,_ 000 change
