   238	
   239	Listing 1 provides (a) a minimal example of a CMake-based build
   240	system with (b) a change set being applied. The example highlights portions of the build specifications from CMakeLists.txt
   241	and SetupFeatures.cmake files. The example build system is inspired by the ET-Legacy project [2] with simplifications such as moving and de-conditionalizing commands and renaming variables.
   242	In CMake, by default, the entry point into the build system
   243	is the CMakeLists.txt file. Commands within the build files are
   244	evaluated sequentially to configure the build process. The cmake
   245	build tool processes the example build system a follows:
   246	(1) The commands in the CMakeLists.txt file are evaluated until
   247	it encounters the file command (line 2). This command defines
   248	the SRC variable, which stores the list of files that match the
   249	specified patterns.
   250	(2) The process reaches the add_executable command (line 4),
   251	where an executable target named server is defined, which
   252	depends on the dereferenced values of SRC (from Step 1).
   253	(3) The include command (line 6) directs the build process to the
   254	SetupFeatures.cmake file, such that the commands in this file
   255	are processed within the same scope before continuing with
   256	the remaining commands in CMakeLists.txt.
   257	(4) In the updated build system, the SetupFeatures.cmake file is
   258	modified by adding a list command (line 2). This command
   259	appends the values stored in IRC_FILES to SRC (from Step 1).
   260	(5) After processing SetupFeatures.cmake, execution returns to
   261	CMakeLists.txt at line 7. The add_executable command (line 8),
   262	declares a new executable target named client, which depends
   263	on the dereferenced values of SRC (from Step 4 in the updated
   264	version and Step 1 in the prior version).
   265	In this example, changes made to SetupFeatures.cmake that
   266	update the SRC variable will not impact the server executable, but
   267	will impact the client executable. This example illustrates how
