   281	changes to build specifications may impact the build process in
   282	non-trivial ways. For instance, if the goal is to add a feature to both
   283	the server and client executables, the incomplete impact of the
   284	change might not be immediately apparent to the developer.
   285	
   286	
   287	**3** **RELATED WORK**
   288	
   289	
   290	In this section, we position our work with respect to the literature
   291	on build system maintenance and change impact analysis.
   292	**Maintenance of Build Systems.** Recent work has focused on
   293	supporting the maintenance of build systems through automated
   294	defect detection [ 22 – 27 ] and repair [ 28 – 30 ]. For example, Macho
   295	et al. [ 27 ] proposed an approach that incorporates method calls
   296	within the source code into the dependency graph to detect external
   297	dependency conflicts in a Java project. Hirebuild [ 29 ] was proposed
   298	to fix build breakages by extracting fixing patterns from historical
   299	data and successfully repaired 45% of their studied breakages. While
   300	the prior work proposes promising directions, they have not yet
   301	been implemented into practice-ready tools due to their limited
   302	effectiveness [ 30 ] and performance [ 31, 32 ]. As a result, quality
   303	assurance of build systems is still predominantly a manual process,
   304	imposing a substantial cognitive load on build maintainers [13].
   305	Another recent line of work assists build maintenance in other
   306	
   307	ways. For example, approaches have been proposed to visualize the
   308	dependency graph to clarify interdependencies between components [ 33, 34 ], automate common refactoring tasks, such as renaming and removing targets [ 22, 35 ], detect the addition and removal
   309	of dependencies [ 35, 36 ], and summarize reports of build failures for
   310	debugging purposes [ 34, 37 ]. While these approaches offer valuable
   311	support, they are mostly concerned with guiding or automating the
   312	application of build system changes, whereas in this paper, we propose an approach that supports developers and reviewers to understand the implications of maintenance activities on build systems.
   313	Macho et al. [ 38 ] stated that not only assisting the application
   314	of the change is necessary, but it is also essential to understand the
   315	details of the changes in build systems after they are applied. They
   316	proposed BuildDiff, a tool that computes the differences between
   317	pairs of Maven build specifications and detects the operation type,
   318	e.g., adding or deleting a dependency or updating the version of
   319	an artifact, to provide detailed information about what has been
   320	modified. However, their approach is not concerned with the implications the changes may have on other portions of the build system.
   321	In the build systems of projects with compile-time variability, the
   322	logic that establishes dependencies among internal and external
   323	sources can propagate in complex ways. In our study, we propose
   324	an approach that detects the impact of changes across the build
   325	system, facilitating change comprehension and review.
   326	**Change Impact Analysis.** Change Impact Analysis (CIA) is the
   327	process of identifying and assessing the consequences of a change
   328	to the software to reveal their broader implications [ 39 ]. While
   329	CIA can be applied to a broad range of software artifacts, studies
   330	predominantly focus on code-based CIA techniques [ 40 ]. These
